.TH SSH2RSATEST 1
.SH NAME
ssh2rsatest \- regression test for ssh2rsa(1)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ssh2rsatest
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Ssh2rsatest
loads
.BR /dis/auth/ssh2rsa.dis 's
in-process test seam,
.BR run(argv,\ input) .
No prebuilt binary and no plan9port copy of
.IR ssh2rsa (1)
exist on this host (see its own BUGS) - so instead of a binary oracle,
this test generates a FRESH real OpenSSH key every run via
.B #C
.RI ( ssh-keygen ),
strips the PEM armor and base64-decodes it (also via
.BR #C ,
the real pipeline's own
.IR pemdecode (1)
job - see
.IR ssh2rsa (1)'s
own DESCRIPTION), and feeds the raw binary blob straight to
.BR run() .
.PP
Correctness is then checked INDEPENDENTLY of any Plan 9 code, using
this tree's own
.B module/ipints.m
arithmetic directly: the extracted
.RI ( n ,
.IR ek ,
.IR dk ,
.IR p ,
.IR q )
must satisfy
.I p*q==n
and
.IR "ek*dk === 1 (mod p\-1)"
and
.IR "(mod q\-1)"
- the defining identities for a valid RSA private exponent and modulus
factorization, not a comparison against anyone else's implementation,
re-derived live every run rather than replayed from a fixed session.
(This does not re-verify the precomputed CRT values
.IR kp / kq / c2 ,
which come from
.B crypt->rsafill()
itself, not from this file's own extraction code - those were checked
once by hand with Python during development, cross-checked against
.BR "openssl rsa -text" 's
own independent decoding of the same key material - see
.IR doc/plan9-port.md
for that derivation.)
.PP
One case generates an encrypted (passphrase-protected) key and
confirms it is correctly rejected with
.BR "private key seems encrypted" ,
matching the source - this translation has no decryption support at
all, by design (see
.IR ssh2rsa (1)).
.SH SOURCE
.B appl/cmd/auth/ssh2rsatest.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR ssh2rsa (1)
.SH BUGS
Depends on a working
.B ssh-keygen
on the host (via
.BR #C );
if it is missing, the affected cases fail loudly with a diagnostic
rather than silently skipping.
